<Note
  track="math"
  slug="norms-distance-cosine"
  lede="A norm measures how big a vector is, a distance measures how far apart two vectors are, and cosine similarity measures only whether they point the same way. The three answer different questions, and choosing the wrong one is the most common mistake in “find me similar things” systems. This note gives you the pictures, the numbers and the rule for choosing."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="why">Why this note matters</h2>
  <p>
    The vectors note ended with word vectors: lists of numbers where similar words get similar lists. The obvious next
    question is “how do I measure <em>how</em> similar?”, and there are three honest answers: how big the dot product
    is, how far apart the two points are, and whether the two arrows point the same way. They rank things differently.
    Every system that searches by meaning, from “find documents like this question” to recommendations, ends with one
    line of code that picks one of the three, and picking without knowing why is how people ship search that returns
    long documents instead of relevant ones. Tomorrow’s <a href="/notes/ml/feature-scaling-encoding/">feature scaling
    note</a> needs the distance half of this, and “how big is this vector?” comes back in week 3 as a way to keep a
    model’s weights small.
  </p>
  <p>Interview questions that are really this note:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Cosine similarity or dot product for <Term id="embedding">embeddings</Term>? When would they rank differently?”</li>
    <li>“Why do we normalize embeddings before storing them for search?”</li>
    <li>“Is a cosine similarity of 0.3 high or low?”</li>
    <li>“What is the difference between L1 and L2 <Term id="regularization">regularization</Term>, and why does L1 produce zeros?” (Week 3; the picture is here.)</li>
    <li>“What does <Term id="gradient-clipping">gradient clipping</Term> do, exactly?” (Week 6; the arithmetic is here.)</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
    <p>
      The L1, L2 and L∞ norms, Euclidean and Manhattan distance, cosine similarity, normalization, and what happens in
      high dimensions. No metric-space theory, no other similarity measures (Jaccard, Mahalanobis) beyond a mention.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Think of each vector as an arrow from a shared starting point. Three questions you can ask:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How long is this arrow?</strong> That is its <em>norm</em>. A vector of all small numbers has a small norm;
      make every number bigger and the norm grows.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How far apart are the two arrow tips?</strong> That is the <em>distance</em> between the vectors. Two long
      arrows pointing the same way have tips close together; a long arrow and a short one pointing the same way do not.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Do the arrows point the same way, ignoring length?</strong> That is <em>cosine similarity</em>. A short
      arrow and a long arrow in the same direction score a perfect 1, even though their tips are far apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      Similarity search is choosing which of these three questions you actually mean.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Everyday idea</th><th>Technical term</th><th>Symbol or code</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>How long the arrow is</td><td><span class="term">norm</span>, specifically the <span class="term">L2 norm</span></td><td><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert" /> or <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_2" />; <code>np.linalg.norm(a)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total of the absolute values</td><td><span class="term">L1 norm</span> (Manhattan, taxicab)</td><td><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_1" />; <code>norm(a, ord=1)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The single biggest entry</td><td><span class="term">L∞ norm</span> (max norm)</td><td><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_\infty" />; <code>norm(a, ord=np.inf)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Straight-line gap between two tips</td><td><span class="term">Euclidean distance</span></td><td><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\rVert" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gap walking along the grid</td><td><span class="term">Manhattan distance</span></td><td><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\rVert_1" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Same direction, length ignored</td><td><span class="term">cosine similarity</span></td><td><Math tex="\cos\theta = \dfrac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert}" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shrink or stretch to length 1</td><td><span class="term">L2-normalize</span></td><td><Math tex="\mathbf{a}/\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert" />; <code>a / np.linalg.norm(a)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cap how long a vector may be</td><td>clipping by norm</td><td><code>a * min(1, c / norm(a))</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="norm">The norm: how big a vector is</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      The length of the arrow (3, 4) is 5, by the right-triangle rule you learned at school: square the sides, add,
      take the square root. In more dimensions there is no triangle to draw, but the rule keeps working: square every
      entry, add them all, square-root. That number is the norm. It is zero only for the all-zero vector, it doubles
      when you double the vector, and it ignores signs because squaring removes them.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>The <span class="term">L2 norm</span> (also Euclidean norm, length, or magnitude) of a vector is</p>
  <Math block tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_2 = \sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2} = \sqrt{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}}" />
  <p>
    When the subscript is missing, <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert" /> means the L2 norm. Three worked values:
    <Math tex="\lVert(3, 4)\rVert = 5" />; <Math tex="\lVert(1, -2, 2)\rVert = \sqrt{1 + 4 + 4} = 3" />;
    <Math tex="\lVert(1, 1, 1, 1)\rVert = 2" />. Four properties, each with a one-line reason:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never negative, and zero only for the zero vector.</strong> Squares cannot be negative, and the sum is zero only when every entry is.</li>
    <li><strong>Scaling.</strong> <Math tex="\lVert c\,\mathbf{a}\rVert = |c|\,\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert" />. Doubling the arrow doubles its length; flipping it leaves the length alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Triangle inequality.</strong> <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}\rVert \le \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert" />. Walking two arrows tip to tail is never shorter than the straight line.</li>
    <li><strong>The squared norm is a dot product.</strong> <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 = \mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}" />, a sum of squares with no square root. Mean squared error is one of these: the squared length of the vector of errors, divided by the count. Sums of squares have very simple slopes (week 2), which is why so many losses and penalties are built from them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The norm of a model’s weight vector is “how big the weights are”. The norm of the step that training is about to
    take is “how big a step”. Both get controlled directly later on: week 3 adds a penalty on the first to fight
    <Term id="overfitting">overfitting</Term>; week 6 caps the second so that one bad batch cannot throw training off.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “The L2 norm is the square root of the sum of squares, equivalently the square root of the vector’s dot product
      with itself. It is non-negative, scales linearly, and satisfies the triangle inequality. Its square is a plain
      sum of squares, which is why squared error and L2 penalties are so convenient to work with.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="other-norms">L1, L2 and L∞</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      “How big is this vector?” has more than one sensible answer. You could add up the absolute values, as if walking
      along city blocks. You could take the straight-line length. Or you could just report the largest single entry.
      All three agree that the zero vector has size zero and that doubling the vector doubles its size; they disagree
      about everything in between, and the disagreement shows up as different shapes for “all the vectors of size 1”.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={1} caption="Every vector of norm 1, under three norms. L1 gives a diamond, L2 a circle, L∞ a square. The corners of the diamond lie on the axes, which is why L1 penalties push weights to exactly zero.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 290" role="img" aria-label="A diamond, a circle and a square centred at the origin, the unit balls of the L1, L2 and L-infinity norms">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(balls)}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={poly(balls, [[1, 1], [-1, 1], [-1, -1], [1, -1]])} stroke-dasharray="7 5" stroke-width="2"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={poly(balls, circle)} stroke-width="3"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={poly(balls, [[1, 0], [0, 1], [-1, 0], [0, -1]])} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(balls, 0)} cy={sy(balls, 1)} r="5"></circle>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(balls, 0.6)} cy={sy(balls, 0.8)} r="5"></circle>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(balls, 0.62)} y={sy(balls, -1.2)}>L∞ = 1 (square)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(balls, 0.75)} y={sy(balls, -0.85)}>L2 = 1 (circle)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(balls, -1.45)} y={sy(balls, -1.25)}>L1 = 1 (diamond)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(balls, -0.08)} y={sy(balls, 1.2)} text-anchor="end">(0, 1): on an axis</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(balls, 0.7)} y={sy(balls, 0.72)}>(0.6, 0.8)</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="90">(3, 4):</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="116">L1 = 3 + 4 = 7</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="142">L2 = √(9 + 16) = 5</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="168">L∞ = max(3, 4) = 4</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="210">always L∞ ≤ L2 ≤ L1</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>The three norms you will meet, all for the example <Math tex="\mathbf{a} = (3, -4)" />:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Norm</th><th>Formula</th><th>For (3, −4)</th><th>Picture of “size 1”</th><th>Where it appears</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>L1</td><td><Math tex="\sum_i |a_i|" /></td><td>3 + 4 = 7</td><td>diamond</td><td>mean absolute error (week 2); the L1 penalty that zeroes out weights (week 3)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>L2</td><td><Math tex="\sqrt{\sum_i a_i^2}" /></td><td>√25 = 5</td><td>circle</td><td>distances, cosine, mean squared error; the L2 penalty on weights (week 3); capping training steps (week 6)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>L∞</td><td><Math tex="\max_i |a_i|" /></td><td>4</td><td>square</td><td>“the largest change in any single weight”; rarely needed, know the name</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    These are all members of one family, the <span class="term">Lp norms</span>
    <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_p = (\sum_i |a_i|^p)^{1/p}" />, with <Math tex="p = 1" />, <Math tex="2" /> and
    <Math tex="\infty" />. Only those three matter here. The important difference between L1 and L2 is visible in
    Fig. 1 and is the answer to a classic interview question. The L1 diamond has <em>corners on the axes</em>, where
    one coordinate is exactly zero. In week 3 you will add a penalty to the loss that says “keep the weights small”.
    If “small” is measured with L1, the best weights tend to land on a corner of the diamond, which means some weights
    become exactly zero and the model becomes <span class="term">sparse</span>: it ignores some features entirely. If
    “small” is measured with L2, the circle has no corners, so all weights shrink towards zero smoothly but rarely
    become exactly zero. The <a href="/notes/ml/regularization/">regularization note</a> draws the full picture; the
    shape of the ball is the half you need now.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norm_(mathematics)"
    title="Wikipedia: Norm (mathematics)"
    meta="Only the sections “Euclidean norm”, “Taxicab norm” and “Maximum norm”, and the picture of unit circles for different p. Ignore the general definitions."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “L1 is the sum of absolute values, L2 the square root of the sum of squares, L∞ the maximum absolute entry.
      The L1 unit ball is a diamond with corners on the axes, which is why an L1 penalty yields sparse weights; the L2
      ball is a sphere, so an L2 penalty shrinks weights without zeroing them.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="distance">Distance between two vectors</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Two points on a map: (1, 2) and (4, 6). A crow flies the straight line between them; a taxi drives along the
      streets, 3 blocks east then 4 blocks north. The crow’s distance is the length of the arrow from one point to the
      other, 5. The taxi’s is 3 + 4 = 7. Distance is always “the norm of the difference”, and which norm you use decides
      whether you are the crow or the taxi.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={2} caption="Distance is the norm of the difference vector a − b = (3, 4). Euclidean (L2) distance is the straight line, 5. Manhattan (L1) distance follows the grid, 3 + 4 = 7.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Two points with a straight line between them and a grid path, labelled with the two distances">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(dist)}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(dist, [1, 2], [4, 2])} stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(dist, [4, 2], [4, 6])} stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(dist, [1, 2], [4, 6])} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(dist, 1)} cy={sy(dist, 2)} r="6"></circle>
        <circle class="stroke fill-paper" cx={sx(dist, 4)} cy={sy(dist, 6)} r="6"></circle>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(dist, 1) - 10} y={sy(dist, 2) + 22} text-anchor="end">b = (1, 2)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(dist, 4) + 10} y={sy(dist, 6) - 6}>a = (4, 6)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(dist, 1.9)} y={sy(dist, 4.4)} text-anchor="end">‖a − b‖₂ = 5</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(dist, 2.5)} y={sy(dist, 2) + 22} text-anchor="middle">3</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(dist, 4) + 10} y={sy(dist, 4)}>4</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="90">a − b = (4 − 1, 6 − 2) = (3, 4)</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="440" y="130">Euclidean: √(3² + 4²) = 5</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="160">Manhattan: |3| + |4| = 7</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="200">crow vs taxi</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    The <span class="term">Euclidean distance</span> between <Math tex="\mathbf{a}" /> and <Math tex="\mathbf{b}" /> is
    the L2 norm of their difference:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="d(\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b}) = \lVert\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}\rVert_2 = \sqrt{\sum_i (a_i - b_i)^2}" />
  <p>
    and the <span class="term">Manhattan distance</span> is the L1 norm of the difference,
    <Math tex="\sum_i |a_i - b_i|" />. Notice that distance depends on <em>where the tips are</em>, not on where the
    arrows point. Two vectors in exactly the same direction can be far apart if one is longer, and that is the whole
    difference between distance and cosine, coming next.
  </p>
  <p>
    One identity ties distance to the dot product and is worth being able to derive on a whiteboard. Expand the
    squared distance with the dot product rules from the <a href="/notes/math/vectors-dot-product/">vectors note</a>:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\lVert\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = (\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b})\cdot(\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}) = \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 - 2\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert^2" />
  <p>
    So for a fixed pair of lengths, a <em>bigger dot product means a smaller distance</em>. When both vectors have
    length 1 this becomes <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = 2 - 2\cos\theta" />, which says that for
    unit vectors, ranking by Euclidean distance and ranking by cosine similarity give the same order. Every system
    that stores vectors for search relies on that fact.
  </p>
  <p>
    Euclidean distance is what the “copy the answers of the most similar examples” methods of week 4
    (<Term id="knn">k-nearest neighbours</Term> and <Term id="k-means">k-means</Term>) use by default, and the mean
    squared error you already know is the squared Euclidean distance between the vector of predictions and the vector
    of true values, divided by the count.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/geometry/hs-geo-analytic-geometry/hs-geo-distance-and-midpoints/v/distance-formula"
    title="Khan Academy: The distance formula"
    meta="Only if the 2-D case is rusty. Five minutes, then do the exercise set under it."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Euclidean distance is the L2 norm of the difference vector. Expanding the square gives
      <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 - 2\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}" />, so for
      unit vectors distance and cosine similarity induce the same ranking.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="cosine">Cosine similarity: direction only</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Two people describe a film. One writes three sentences, the other writes three pages, but they make the same
      points in the same proportions. As vectors of “how much they said about each theme”, one is simply a longer copy
      of the other: same direction, different length. Distance says they are far apart; the dot product is big because
      of the long review; but cosine similarity says 1.0, identical in direction. When you want to compare
      <em>what</em> is being said and not <em>how much</em>, cosine is the measure.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    <span class="term">Cosine similarity</span> is the dot product with both lengths divided out, which by the
    geometry of the <a href="/notes/math/vectors-dot-product/#geometry">previous note</a> is the cosine of the angle
    between the vectors:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\,\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert}" />
  <p>
    It is always between −1 and 1. It is 1 for the same direction, 0 for orthogonal, −1 for opposite. And it is
    <em>scale-invariant</em>: multiply either vector by any positive number and the cosine does not move.
  </p>
  <Figure n={3} caption="a = (1, 2), b = (2, 4) and c = (2, 1). By cosine, b is identical to a (same direction) and c is less similar (0.8). By distance, c is the closer one (1.41 vs 2.24). Neither is wrong; they answer different questions.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 310" role="img" aria-label="Three arrows from the origin: a and b pointing the same way with b twice as long, and c at an angle, with distances and cosines labelled">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(cosP)}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(cosP, [0, 0], [2, 4])} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={arrow(cosP, [0, 0], [1, 2])} stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={arrow(cosP, [0, 0], [2, 1])} stroke-width="2.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={arrow(cosP, [1, 2], [2, 4])} stroke-dasharray="4 4"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={arrow(cosP, [1, 2], [2, 1])} stroke-dasharray="4 4"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(cosP, 1) - 10} y={sy(cosP, 2) - 6} text-anchor="end">a = (1, 2)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(cosP, 2) + 8} y={sy(cosP, 4)}>b = (2, 4)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(cosP, 2) + 8} y={sy(cosP, 1) + 4}>c = (2, 1)</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(cosP, 1.65)} y={sy(cosP, 3.2)} text-anchor="end">gap 2.24</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(cosP, 1.6)} y={sy(cosP, 1.3)}>gap 1.41</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="80">cos(a, b) = 10 / (√5 · √20) = 1.0</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="106">cos(a, c) = 4 / (√5 · √5) = 0.8</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="440" y="150">by cosine: b wins</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="190">‖a − b‖ = ‖(−1, −2)‖ = 2.24</text>
      <text class="small" x="440" y="216">‖a − c‖ = ‖(−1, 1)‖ = 1.41</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="440" y="260">by distance: c wins</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Work the numbers in Fig. 3 once by hand; they are the kind an interviewer asks for.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 2 + 8 = 10" />, <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert = \sqrt{5}" />, <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert = \sqrt{20}" />, so <Math tex="\cos = 10/\sqrt{100} = 1" />.</li>
    <li><Math tex="\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{c} = 2 + 2 = 4" />, <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{c}\rVert = \sqrt{5}" />, so <Math tex="\cos = 4/5 = 0.8" />, an angle of about 37°.</li>
    <li><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{b}\rVert = \lVert(-1, -2)\rVert = \sqrt{5} = 2.24" /> and <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a} - \mathbf{c}\rVert = \lVert(-1, 1)\rVert = \sqrt{2} = 1.41" />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You will also meet <span class="term">cosine distance</span>, defined as <Math tex="1 - \cos\theta" />, which runs
    from 0 (same direction) to 2 (opposite) and lets “smaller is closer” libraries treat cosine like a distance. It is
    the same information with the sign flipped.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="watch"
    href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e9U0QAFbfLI"
    title="StatQuest: Cosine Similarity, Clearly Explained"
    meta="About 10 minutes. Uses word counts in short and long documents, which is exactly the film-review story, and works the arithmetic on screen."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Cosine similarity is the dot product divided by both norms, so it is the cosine of the angle between the
      vectors: it lies in [−1, 1] and is invariant to scaling either vector. It measures direction only, which is what
      you want when vector length reflects something irrelevant such as document length.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="which">Dot product, cosine or distance?</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Here is a tiny search engine with one query and three documents, in two dimensions so you can see it. Score the
      documents three ways and you get three different winners. Nothing is broken; each measure is answering its own
      question, and the engineer’s job is to know which question the product is asking.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={4} caption="One query, three documents, three rankings. Dot product favours the long d₁. Cosine ranks by angle: d₁, d₂, d₃. Euclidean distance favours the nearby point d₂.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 310" role="img" aria-label="A query arrow and three document arrows from the origin, with a table of dot product, cosine and distance scores and the resulting rankings">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={axes(rank)}></path>
        {docs.map((d) => (
          <path class={`stroke ${d.red ? 'ink-red' : ''}`} d={arrow(rank, [0, 0], d.v)} stroke-width={d.red ? 3.5 : 2.5} marker-end="url(#arrow)" />
        ))}
      </g>
      {docs.map((d) => (
        <text class={`small ${d.red ? 'label-red' : ''}`} x={sx(rank, d.v[0]) + (d.name === 'd₂' ? -78 : 8)} y={sy(rank, d.v[1]) + (d.name === 'd₂' ? -16 : 4)}>
          {d.name} ({d.v[0]}, {d.v[1]})
        </text>
      ))}
      <text class="small" x="400" y="60" font-weight="700">score</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="60" font-weight="700">d₁</text>
      <text class="small" x="560" y="60" font-weight="700">d₂</text>
      <text class="small" x="620" y="60" font-weight="700">d₃</text>
      <text class="small" x="400" y="90">dot</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="90">8.0</text><text class="small" x="560" y="90">2.0</text><text class="small" x="620" y="90">3.3</text>
      <text class="small" x="400" y="120">cosine</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="120">1.00</text><text class="small" x="560" y="120">0.98</text><text class="small" x="620" y="120">0.77</text>
      <text class="small" x="400" y="150">distance</text>
      <text class="small" x="500" y="150">4.24</text><text class="small" x="560" y="150">0.28</text><text class="small" x="620" y="150">2.12</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="400" y="200">dot: d₁, d₃, d₂</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="400" y="226">cosine: d₁, d₂, d₃</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="400" y="252">distance: d₂, d₃, d₁</text>
      <text class="small" x="400" y="290">three measures, three orders</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    Query <Math tex="\mathbf{q} = (1, 1)" />. Document <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_1 = (4, 4)" /> points exactly like the
    query but is four times longer, like a long article on the right topic. <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_2 = (0.8, 1.2)" />
    is a short document very near the query. <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_3 = (3, 0.3)" /> is long and mostly about
    something else. The dot product rewards length, so it ranks the off-topic <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_3" /> above the
    on-topic <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_2" />. Cosine ranks purely by angle. Euclidean distance rewards being nearby as a
    point, so it demotes the long on-topic <Math tex="\mathbf{d}_1" />.
  </p>
  <p>The rule for choosing:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Measure</th><th>Sensitive to length?</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Typical place</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Dot product</td><td>Yes, rewards long vectors</td><td>Length carries meaning you want, such as popularity or confidence, or vectors are already normalized</td><td>Recommendation scores; <Term id="attention">attention</Term> (week 8); models trained to be compared this way</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cosine</td><td>No</td><td>Only direction matters; lengths vary for irrelevant reasons (document length, word count)</td><td>Searching text by meaning, duplicate detection</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Euclidean</td><td>Yes, as position</td><td>Vectors are points in a space where absolute position matters, usually after feature scaling</td><td>k-nearest neighbours, k-means, tables of numbers</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    And the fact that dissolves most of the debate: <strong>if you L2-normalize every vector first, all three measures
    give the same ranking</strong>. For unit vectors the dot product <em>is</em> the cosine, and by the identity in
    the distance section, Euclidean distance is a decreasing function of it. This is why models that produce
    embeddings usually ship normalized vectors, and why the databases built for storing them offer “cosine” as an
    option: internally they normalize once and then use the cheaper dot product. The honest answer to “cosine or dot?”
    is therefore often “normalize, then it does not matter; if you cannot normalize, decide whether length means
    something”.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-similarity/"
    title="Pinecone: Vector similarity explained"
    meta="A vendor article, but a clear one: Euclidean, cosine and dot product side by side with pictures, and the advice to match the metric to how the embedding model was trained."
  />
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://www.sbert.net/examples/applications/semantic-search/README.html"
    title="Sentence-Transformers: Semantic search"
    meta="Scroll to the paragraph on cosine similarity versus dot product and the note that normalized embeddings make them equivalent. This is the library most search-by-meaning projects start with."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “Dot product rewards vector length, cosine ignores it, Euclidean distance measures position. For text
      embeddings I use cosine, or equivalently L2-normalize and use the dot product, because document length should
      not affect relevance. I would use the raw dot product when the model was trained for it or when magnitude
      encodes something meaningful.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="normalize">Normalizing: three things with one name</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      “Normalize the data” is said in three different situations and means three different operations. One rescales
      a single arrow to length 1. One rescales each <em>column</em> of a data table so that features measured in
      different units become comparable. One rescales each <em>row</em> of numbers flowing through the inside of a
      big model so that training stays stable (that one belongs to week 6, but it is named here so you recognise it).
      People mix these up constantly, including in interviews, so keep the three apart by name.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Name</th><th>What is rescaled</th><th>How</th><th>Why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">L2 normalization</span> (unit vectors)</td>
          <td>One vector</td>
          <td><Math tex="\mathbf{a} / \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_2" />; <code>a / np.linalg.norm(a)</code></td>
          <td>So that dot product = cosine and length cannot bias similarity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="term">Standardization</span> (z-scoring, feature scaling)</td>
          <td>Each feature column of a data table</td>
          <td><Math tex="(x - \text{mean}) / \text{std}" /> per column; <code>StandardScaler</code></td>
          <td>So that a feature in dollars and one in years count equally in distances and in training</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><Term id="batchnorm">LayerNorm</Term> / RMSNorm (week 6)</td>
          <td>Each vector of numbers flowing through a big model, per example</td>
          <td>LayerNorm: subtract the mean, divide by the std of the entries. RMSNorm: divide by <Math tex="\sqrt{\text{mean}(x_i^2)}" /></td>
          <td>So that the size of those numbers stays steady from step to step during training</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two of these are norms in disguise. L2 normalization divides by the L2 norm. RMSNorm divides by the
    <em>root mean square</em> of the entries, which is <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{x}\rVert_2 / \sqrt{d}" />; so RMSNorm,
    used inside most recent language models, is L2 normalization scaled by <Math tex="\sqrt{d}" />, followed by a
    learned per-entry gain. Standardization is the odd one out: it is per column, not per vector, and it belongs to
    the <a href="/notes/ml/feature-scaling-encoding/">feature scaling note</a>, which is next in the reading order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The habit to build: whenever someone says “normalize”, ask “which axis?”. Per vector (a row) or per feature (a
    column)? The answer changes the code and the meaning.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “L2 normalization scales each vector to unit length. Standardization rescales each feature column to zero mean
      and unit variance. LayerNorm and RMSNorm rescale each vector inside a network per example; RMSNorm divides by
      the root mean square, which is the L2 norm over <Math tex="\sqrt{d}" />. They are different operations on
      different axes.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="high-dim">What changes in 768 dimensions</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      In two dimensions, two random arrows are quite likely to point roughly the same way; there are only so many
      directions. In 768 dimensions there are astronomically many directions, and two random arrows are almost
      certainly nearly at right angles. That changes what a cosine score means: a value that would be unremarkable in
      2-D is a strong signal in 768-D. It also means distances between random points all come out about the same,
      which is why nearest-neighbour search in high dimensions needs clever data structures rather than brute force.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Figure n={5} caption="Cosine similarity between pairs of random vectors. In 3 dimensions it spreads over the whole range; in 768 it is piled up within a few hundredths of zero. The spread shrinks like 1/√d.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 312" role="img" aria-label="Three bell-shaped curves centred at zero cosine, becoming narrower as the dimension increases">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${conc.x} ${sy(conc, 0)} H${conc.x + conc.w}`}></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={curve(conc, bump(0.5))} stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 5"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d={curve(conc, bump(0.18))} stroke-width="2.5"></path>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d={curve(conc, bump(0.036), 400)} stroke-width="3"></path>
      </g>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, -1)} y={sy(conc, 0) + 22} text-anchor="middle">−1</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, 0)} y={sy(conc, 0) + 22} text-anchor="middle">0</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, 1)} y={sy(conc, 0) + 22} text-anchor="middle">1</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, 0)} y={sy(conc, 0) + 44} text-anchor="middle">cosine similarity of two random vectors</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, 0.72)} y={sy(conc, 0.4)}>d = 3</text>
      <text class="small" x={sx(conc, 0.28)} y={sy(conc, 0.45)}>d = 30</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x={sx(conc, 0.06)} y={sy(conc, 1.02)}>d = 768: spread ≈ 0.036</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>
    For two random vectors with independent entries, the cosine similarity has mean 0 and a spread (standard
    deviation) of about <Math tex="1/\sqrt{d}" />. In 768 dimensions that is 0.036. Two <em>unrelated</em> embeddings
    would therefore sit within a few hundredths of zero if the embedding space were random, so a cosine of 0.3 is
    already far outside chance. Two practical cautions follow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Thresholds are model-specific.</strong> Real embedding models are not random: many produce vectors that
      all share a common direction, so even unrelated texts can score 0.5 or more. A threshold like “0.8 means
      duplicate” has to be calibrated on your own data for your own model; it does not transfer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Distances concentrate.</strong> In high dimensions the nearest and farthest neighbours of a point are
      often not very different in distance, which makes brute-force nearest-neighbour search both slow and fragile.
      This is one face of the <span class="term">curse of dimensionality</span>, and it is why week 11’s vector
      databases use approximate search structures.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <Math tex="1/\sqrt{d}" /> fact has one more consequence you will meet in week 8: dot products between random
    <Math tex="d" />-dimensional vectors have a spread of about <Math tex="\sqrt{d}" />, which is why
    <Term id="attention">attention</Term> divides its scores by <Math tex="\sqrt{d_k}" />. Problem 14 in the vectors
    note measured exactly this.
  </p>
  <Source
    kind="read"
    href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curse_of_dimensionality"
    title="Wikipedia: Curse of dimensionality"
    meta="Read only the “Distance functions” section. Two paragraphs, and it is the formal statement of “distances concentrate”."
  />
  <Callout kind="say">
    <p>
      “In high dimensions random vectors are nearly orthogonal, with cosine spread about <Math tex="1/\sqrt{d}" />,
      so moderate cosine values are meaningful and distances concentrate. Similarity thresholds must be calibrated per
      embedding model, and exact nearest-neighbour search becomes expensive, which motivates approximate search
      structures.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

