  <h2 id="later">Where you will meet this again</h2>
  <Callout kind="note" label="Optional preview">
    <p>
      A look ahead. Unfamiliar words link to the glossary; skip this on a first read.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tomorrow, feature scaling.</strong> Standardising columns so that Euclidean distance treats every feature
      fairly is the whole point of the next note.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 3, keeping weights small.</strong> <Term id="regularization">Regularization</Term> adds the norm of
      the weight vector to the loss: L2 shrinks every weight; L1 zeroes some out, for the diamond-versus-circle reason
      in Fig. 1.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 4, nearest neighbours and clustering.</strong> <Term id="knn">kNN</Term> and
      <Term id="k-means">k-means</Term> are Euclidean distance plus bookkeeping, and both break on unscaled features.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 6, training tricks.</strong> <Term id="gradient-clipping">Gradient clipping</Term> is the
      “cap the length” code above applied to the training step: almost every modern model is trained with the step’s
      total L2 norm capped at 1. Weight decay is the L2 penalty applied a little at every step. LayerNorm and RMSNorm
      rescale the vector of numbers at each position inside the model; RMSNorm divides by an L2 norm in disguise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 8, attention.</strong> The <Math tex="\sqrt{d_k}" /> in attention exists because dot products of
      <Math tex="d_k" />-dimensional vectors have spread <Math tex="\sqrt{d_k}" />, the fact from the high-dimensions
      section.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Weeks 11 and 12, retrieval.</strong> A <Term id="rag">RAG</Term> system turns document chunks and
      questions into vectors, usually L2-normalizes them, and ranks by cosine as a dot product of unit vectors. The
      database’s “metric” setting is the choice in the <a href="#which">which-measure section</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The most common form is “cosine similarity versus dot product versus Euclidean distance, and which one would you
    use for embeddings?”. Here is a complete answer in about seventy seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “All three compare two vectors but answer different questions. The dot product is the sum of element-wise
      products; it grows with the vectors’ lengths, so a long vector scores highly against everything. Cosine
      similarity divides the dot product by both L2 norms, so it is the cosine of the angle between the vectors, lies
      in minus one to one, and ignores length entirely. Euclidean distance is the L2 norm of the difference and
      measures how far apart the vectors are as points.
    </p>
    <p>
      For text embeddings I default to cosine, because vector length usually reflects things like document length or
      token count that should not affect relevance. In practice I L2-normalize the embeddings once at indexing time
      and use the dot product, which is identical to cosine for unit vectors and cheaper; for unit vectors Euclidean
      distance gives the same ranking too, since the squared distance is two minus twice the cosine. I would keep the
      raw dot product when the embedding model was trained with a dot-product objective or when magnitude carries
      signal, such as confidence or popularity. One caveat: in high dimensions random vectors are nearly orthogonal,
      so what counts as a ‘high’ cosine depends on the model and must be calibrated on your own data.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why does an L1 penalty give sparse weights and L2 not?” The L1 ball has corners on the axes; the best point tends to land on a corner where coordinates are exactly zero. The L2 ball is round.</li>
    <li>“What does clipping by norm do?” Computes the L2 norm of the whole vector; if it exceeds the threshold, scales every entry by threshold/norm so the norm equals the threshold. Direction unchanged, size capped. (Week 6 applies this to training steps.)</li>
    <li>“Is a cosine of 0.3 high?” For random 768-D vectors the spread is about 0.036, so 0.3 is far from chance; but real models have a shared component, so calibrate per model.</li>
    <li>“Cosine distance?” <Math tex="1 - \cos\theta" />, in [0, 2]. Not a true metric (fails the triangle inequality), which some libraries care about.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="Define the L1, L2 and L∞ norms and give all three for (3, −4).">
    <p>Sum of absolute values: 7. Square root of the sum of squares: 5. Largest absolute entry: 4. Always L∞ ≤ L2 ≤ L1.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the relationship between the L2 norm and the dot product?">
    <p><Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert_2 = \sqrt{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}}" />, so the squared norm is the self dot product: a plain sum of squares.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How do you compute the Euclidean distance between two vectors, and how does it relate to the dot product?">
    <p>The L2 norm of the difference. <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 - 2\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}" />: larger dot product, smaller distance, for fixed lengths.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Two vectors have cosine similarity 1 but Euclidean distance 10. How?">
    <p>They point in the same direction but have different lengths, for example (1, 0) and (11, 0). Cosine ignores length; distance does not.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why do vector databases often normalize embeddings and then use the dot product?">
    <p>For unit vectors the dot product equals the cosine similarity and Euclidean distance is a decreasing function of it, so all three rankings agree, and the dot product is the cheapest to compute.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="When would you deliberately keep the raw dot product instead of cosine?">
    <p>When vector length carries meaning you want to rank by (popularity, confidence) or when the embedding model was trained with a dot-product objective and its documentation says to use it.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why does an L1 penalty on the weights lead to sparse models? (Week 3 asks this; the picture is here.)">
    <p>The L1 unit ball is a diamond with corners on the coordinate axes. Keeping the loss small while keeping the L1 norm small tends to land on a corner, where some coordinates are exactly zero. The L2 ball is a sphere with no corners, so L2 shrinks weights smoothly without zeroing them.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What exactly does “clip this vector to norm c” do?">
    <p>It computes the vector’s L2 norm. If that norm exceeds <Math tex="c" />, every entry is multiplied by <Math tex="c / \text{norm}" />, so the norm becomes <Math tex="c" /> and the direction is unchanged. Training code does this to its update step (week 6).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Name three operations people call “normalization” and the axis each acts on.">
    <p>L2 normalization: one vector, scaled to unit length (per row). Standardization: each feature column, to zero mean and unit variance (per column). LayerNorm/RMSNorm: each vector of numbers inside a big model, per example (per row).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is RMSNorm in terms of the L2 norm?">
    <p>It divides a <Math tex="d" />-dimensional vector by <Math tex="\sqrt{\text{mean}(x_i^2)} = \lVert\mathbf{x}\rVert_2/\sqrt{d}" />, then multiplies by a learned gain. It is L2 normalization up to the factor <Math tex="\sqrt{d}" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What happens to the cosine similarity of random vectors as the dimension grows?">
    <p>It concentrates around zero with spread about <Math tex="1/\sqrt{d}" />; in 768 dimensions that is about 0.036. Random high-dimensional vectors are nearly orthogonal.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is cosine distance a true distance metric?">
    <p>No. <Math tex="1 - \cos\theta" /> is non-negative and symmetric but can violate the triangle inequality. Libraries that need a metric use the angle itself or Euclidean distance on normalized vectors instead.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first, then check. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. Compute L1, L2 and L∞ norms of (1, −2, 2), (0, 0, 5), (−1, −1, −1, −1) and (0.6, 0.8).">
    <p>
      (1, −2, 2): 5, 3, 2. (0, 0, 5): 5, 5, 5. (−1, −1, −1, −1): 4, 2, 1. (0.6, 0.8): 1.4, 1, 0.8.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Normalize (2, −2, 1) and (3, 0, 4) to unit length. Then compute their cosine similarity in two ways: from the originals and as a dot product of the unit vectors.">
    <p>
      Norms 3 and 5, so <Math tex="(2/3, -2/3, 1/3)" /> and <Math tex="(0.6, 0, 0.8)" />. Dot product of originals
      <Math tex="6 + 0 + 4 = 10" />, over <Math tex="3 \cdot 5" />, gives 0.667. Unit vectors:
      <Math tex="0.4 + 0 + 0.267 = 0.667" />. Same.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Euclidean and Manhattan distances between (1, 2, 3) and (4, 6, 3), and between (0, 0) and (−3, −4).">
    <p>
      Differences (3, 4, 0): Euclidean 5, Manhattan 7. Differences (−3, −4): Euclidean 5, Manhattan 7. Signs do not
      matter to either.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Verify ‖a − b‖² = ‖a‖² + ‖b‖² − 2 a·b for a = (1, 2) and b = (3, 1).">
    <p>
      Left: <Math tex="\lVert(-2, 1)\rVert^2 = 5" />. Right: <Math tex="5 + 10 - 2 \cdot 5 = 5" />. ✓
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Two unit vectors have cosine similarity 0.5. What is the Euclidean distance between them? And if the cosine is −1?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = 2 - 2(0.5) = 1" />, so distance 1. For cosine −1:
      <Math tex="2 + 2 = 4" />, distance 2, the diameter of the unit sphere.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. Query (2, 1), documents A = (4, 2), B = (1, 1), C = (0, 3). Rank by dot product, cosine and Euclidean distance.">
    <p>
      Dot: A 10, B 3, C 3. Cosine: A 1.0, B <Math tex="3/(\sqrt5\sqrt2) = 0.949" />, C <Math tex="3/(\sqrt5 \cdot 3) = 0.447" />.
      Distance: A <Math tex="\sqrt5 = 2.24" />, B 1, C <Math tex="\sqrt8 = 2.83" />. Rankings: dot A, then B and C tied;
      cosine A, B, C; distance B, A, C.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. A training step wants to move the weights by (3, 4, 12), but the step’s length is capped at 1.0. What step is actually taken?">
    <p>
      Norm <Math tex="\sqrt{9 + 16 + 144} = 13" />. Scale by <Math tex="1/13" />: <Math tex="(0.231, 0.308, 0.923)" />,
      which has norm 1. Same direction, capped size.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. Which points have L1 norm exactly 1 in 2-D? Sketch them. Where on that shape is the point with the most zeros?">
    <p>
      The diamond with corners (1, 0), (0, 1), (−1, 0), (0, −1). The corners have one coordinate equal to zero; every
      other point on the diamond has both coordinates non-zero. That is the geometric reason for L1 sparsity.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. Data column: heights in centimetres (160, 170, 180) and a column of incomes (20,000, 50,000, 80,000). Standardize both columns. Why would k-nearest neighbours be useless without this step?">
    <p>
      Heights: mean 170, std 8.16 → (−1.22, 0, 1.22). Incomes: mean 50,000, std 24,495 → (−1.22, 0, 1.22). Without
      scaling, a 10 cm height difference is 10 units while a 30,000 income difference is 30,000 units, so Euclidean
      distance would depend almost entirely on income. (Population standard deviation used.)
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. RMSNorm with no learned gain is applied to x = (1, 2, 3, 6). What comes out? Check that the result has root mean square 1.">
    <p>
      Mean of squares <Math tex="(1 + 4 + 9 + 36)/4 = 12.5" />, root 3.536. Output
      <Math tex="(0.283, 0.566, 0.849, 1.697)" />. Squares sum to 4, mean 1. ✓
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. In 100 dimensions, roughly what cosine similarity would you expect between two unrelated random vectors, and what range covers most pairs?">
    <p>
      Mean 0, spread about <Math tex="1/\sqrt{100} = 0.1" />. Most pairs fall within about ±0.2. A cosine of 0.5 would
      be five spreads away from chance.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. Show that cosine similarity is unchanged when a is replaced by 3a, and flips sign when a is replaced by −a.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(3\mathbf{a})\cdot\mathbf{b} / (\lVert 3\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert) = 3\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} / (3\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert)" />,
      the 3s cancel. With −1 the numerator flips sign but the norm uses <Math tex="|{-1}| = 1" />, so the cosine flips.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Implement norms for p = 1, 2, ∞, Euclidean and Manhattan distance, and cosine similarity, with plain Python loops. Test against NumPy on random vectors, including the property L∞ ≤ L2 ≤ L1.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def norm(a, p=2):
    if p == 1:   return sum(abs(x) for x in a)
    if p == 2:   return sum(x * x for x in a) ** 0.5
    if p == float("inf"): return max(abs(x) for x in a)
    raise ValueError(p)

def distance(a, b, p=2):     return norm([x - y for x, y in zip(a, b)], p)
def cosine(a, b):            return sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / (norm(a) * norm(b))

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
for _ in range(500):
    a, b = rng.normal(size=20), rng.normal(size=20)
    assert np.isclose(norm(a, 1), np.linalg.norm(a, 1))
    assert np.isclose(norm(a, 2), np.linalg.norm(a))
    assert np.isclose(norm(a, float("inf")), np.linalg.norm(a, np.inf))
    assert norm(a, float("inf")) <= norm(a, 2) <= norm(a, 1)
    assert np.isclose(distance(a, b), np.linalg.norm(a - b))
    assert np.isclose(distance(a, b, 1), np.abs(a - b).sum())
    assert np.isclose(cosine(a, b), (a @ b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b)))
print("ok")`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Load the iris dataset, pick one flower as the query, and find its 5 nearest neighbours by Euclidean distance (a) on raw features and (b) after standardizing each column. Compare the two neighbour lists.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from sklearn.datasets import load_iris

X = load_iris().data                       # (150, 4): four measurements in centimetres
q_idx = 10
def neighbours(M):
    d = np.linalg.norm(M - M[q_idx], axis=1)
    return np.argsort(d)[1:6]              # skip index 0, which is the query itself

raw = neighbours(X)
Xs = (X - X.mean(axis=0)) / X.std(axis=0)  # standardize each COLUMN (axis=0)
std = neighbours(Xs)
print("raw         :", raw)
print("standardized:", std)
print("overlap     :", len(set(raw) & set(std)), "of 5")
# Iris features are all in cm with similar ranges, so the lists overlap a lot.
# Repeat with X[:, 0] *= 100 (pretend one column is in metres x 100) and watch the raw list collapse
# to whichever flowers match on that one column.`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="15. Sentence embeddings without a model: represent 6 short sentences as bag-of-words count vectors, compute pairwise dot products and cosine similarities, and show one pair where the two measures disagree about which sentence is most similar to the first.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from collections import Counter

sents = ["the cat sat on the mat",
         "the cat sat on the mat the cat sat on the mat the cat sat on the mat",   # same words, three times
         "a dog sat on a rug",
         "the cat",
         "stocks fell sharply on the news",
         "the mat"]
vocab = sorted({w for s in sents for w in s.split()})
V = np.array([[Counter(s.split())[w] for w in vocab] for s in sents], dtype=float)   # (6, len(vocab))

dot = V @ V[0]
cos = dot / (np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1) * np.linalg.norm(V[0]))
for i, s in enumerate(sents):
    print(f"{i}: dot={dot[i]:5.1f}  cos={cos[i]:.2f}  {s[:40]}")
# Sentence 1 (the triple repeat) has the largest dot product by far because it is long,
# but its cosine with sentence 0 is exactly 1.0, the same as... itself: direction is identical.
# Sentence 3 ("the cat") has a small dot product but a high cosine. Cosine is the sensible measure here.`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="16. Several arrays together form one long vector (think: all the weights of a model, in pieces). Write clip_by_global_norm(arrays, max_norm) that treats them as one vector, caps its L2 norm, and leaves the direction unchanged. Test that the joint norm afterwards is exactly max_norm, that nothing happens when it was already small, and that every array was scaled by the same factor.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def clip_by_global_norm(arrays, max_norm):
    total = np.sqrt(sum((a ** 2).sum() for a in arrays))     # norm of all the pieces stacked into one vector
    scale = min(1.0, max_norm / (total + 1e-12))             # only shrink, never grow
    return [a * scale for a in arrays], total

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
pieces = [rng.normal(size=(3, 3)) * 5, rng.normal(size=(4,)) * 5]
clipped, before = clip_by_global_norm(pieces, 1.0)
after = np.sqrt(sum((a ** 2).sum() for a in clipped))
print(round(before, 2), round(after, 6))                     # (large) 1.0
assert np.isclose(after, 1.0)
assert np.allclose(clipped[0] / pieces[0], clipped[1][0] / pieces[1][0])   # same factor everywhere: direction kept

small = [np.array([0.1, 0.2])]
assert np.allclose(clip_by_global_norm(small, 1.0)[0][0], small[0])        # already short: untouched
print("ok")
# In week 6 this exact function, applied to the training step, is called gradient clipping.`}
    />
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Using the dot product on unnormalized embeddings and wondering why long documents always win.</strong>
      Length leaks into the score. Normalize, or use cosine.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Treating a cosine of 0.9 as “very similar” for every model.</strong> Some embedding models give 0.7 to
      unrelated texts. Calibrate thresholds on your own labelled pairs.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Normalizing along the wrong axis.</strong> <code>np.linalg.norm(E, axis=0)</code> on an (n, d) matrix
      measures each <em>feature column</em>, not each vector. For one length per row, and so for unit vectors, use
      <code>axis=1</code> with <code>keepdims=True</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Confusing standardization with L2 normalization.</strong> One is per column (features), the other per row
      (vectors). Applying the wrong one to tabular data or to embeddings silently destroys the information you needed.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Running k-nearest neighbours on raw features with different units.</strong> The feature with the biggest
      numbers decides every distance. Standardize first.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Saying “an L2 penalty makes weights zero”.</strong> It shrinks them towards zero; L1 is the one that
      makes them exactly zero. The diamond versus the circle.
    </p>
  </Callout>

